diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** None — all subsystems are currently healthy and inter-campus traffic flows correctly in both directions.

**Mechanism (why the report exists):** The helpdesk ticket describes a complete campus isolation event that cannot be reproduced or corroborated by any live evidence collected today. The user's description ("same as last time," "that box") suggests a recurring transient that may have self-resolved or was already resolved before diagnosis began.

**What was checked:** (1) `infra_sweep` across all 28 devices — zero ACL/firewall rules, all 15 host endpoints properly addressed with default routes, no static ARP poisoning, no resolver divergence. (2) `l2_snapshot` — zero duplicate MAC groups; all flagged DOWN interfaces are documented `unused_*` ports. (3) `ospf_snapshot` — all 9 FRR routers are healthy (vtysh and control sockets responsive); every router holds Full adjacencies and carries all 17 expected OSPF prefixes (10 WAN/core + 7 campus subnets), meaning the routing fabric is fully converged. (4) Direct bidirectional ping `pc5 (172.16.10.5) → pc1 (10.5.0.1)` returned 0% loss at 2.6–5.9 ms, confirming the mswitch0-campus → sshswitch-campus direction that the Phase 1 reachability sweep had not exercised. (5) `mswitch0` FRR routing table confirmed all sshswitch-campus prefixes (10.5.0.0/24, 10.6.0.0/24, etc.) resolve correctly via router16. (6) `pressure_sweep` found no stress tools, no CPU/socket pressure on any device.

**Root cause:** No fault is present in the current network state; the reported campus-wide outage is unconfirmed and has left no mechanism or trace evidence.

**Fix:** No corrective action is needed for the current state. If the outage recurs, collect OSPF neighbor logs (`show ip ospf neighbor`) and interface carrier-change counters on `router16`, `router17`, `mswitch0`, and `sshswitch` immediately at the time of the event to identify whether an OSPF adjacency drop or link flap is the trigger.
